CORE
main.c
--unwind 8 --rounds 5 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
The thread-creation bound (`--thread-creation-bound`, default 2) stops a
recognised "spawn N threads in a loop" after k threads. That is sound for
counterexamples -- the k-thread program is a real execution of the N-thread
one -- but it must not cut a family the program could exhaust anyway.

Here four handles are declared, four threads created, and only three joined,
so the race is between the fourth thread -- never joined, still running -- and
main's unguarded read of `data`. At the default bound only two threads exist,
every one of them is joined, the unjoined-thread race cannot occur, and the
answer was SUCCESSFUL on a program that plainly races. The same shape is
`pthread-race-challenges/thread-join-array-const-race`, which this cost -32 on
(SV-COMP scores a missed bug at -32 against +1 for finding it).

Climbing `--unwind` never helped and that was the confusing part: the binding
bound was a different one, independent of the loop bound, and the thread count
stayed at two from `--unwind 4` to `--unwind 64`.

The bound is now taken from the declared length of the thread-handle array
when that is small enough to unwind in full. The analysis has already proved
every mention of the array lives inside the recognised loops, so its declared
length is exactly how many threads the family can create. The 10000-element
goblint families this analysis was written for are far above the ceiling and
stay bounded, which is what keeps them finishing at all.

The companion fix is in the reporting: `thread_creation_bound_hit` was set and
read by nobody, so where the bound does still truncate the run said nothing at
normal verbosity and a vacuous safe answer looked exactly like a proof. It now
prints "Thread creation incomplete: ...", in the same style as "Unwinding
incomplete" and with the same care not to contain the literal verdict strings
callers grep for.
